At the centre of the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]]'s city stands the tower that carries the strider's gyroscope. The hub is laid out in rings: the fanes at the middle, a path for travel about them, then the shared buildings such as [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Giacomo's|Giacomo's]], the gym and the general store. Inside those stands the tower itself, a sheer shaft of yellow-gold metal rising some five hundred feet, with no windows and no obvious door.

At its top floats the great gyroscopic structure, the strider's helm: many rings turning within one another, with scoop-shaped cups rotating about the edges and smaller rings and arms narrowing inward. [[Org/Belvedere/index|Belvedere]] would need great capital and manpower to build its like. Magic gathers near its top, and machinery sounds within the tower. No controls stand at the top of the tower, only the gyroscope, and no one has said how the helm is piloted.

The tower is entered from below. Past the lit halls of the lower decks, a passage ends at a balcony ten feet square that looks into a great cylindrical shaft. A platform fifteen feet tall hangs in the middle of the shaft on heavy cables that run up into the gyroscope, and the bridges that once joined the balconies to it have been torn away and left hanging. The platform holds a few small rooms floored and walled in metal grating, with doors made in halves that open by pushing one half down and the other up. Papers lie scattered across the floors: a geological survey of country no one has placed, a list of dates on starward worlds, the first visible entry seven years old and numbered star 3,415,612, running down to zero six months ago, and a requisition form, denied for shortage, for three barrels of flugistim, a fuel for the spire. The metal about the base of the spire has more give to it than the rest of the strider's, soft enough to press a hand into, and [[Bestiary/Fiends|corrupted Harlequins]] keep to the rooms.

[[Org/Amber Call/People/Silven Grayson|Grayson]] says the helm is not in the ruined town at the prow, whatever the Party assumed. The whole central circle about the tower, Giacomo's included, is now neutral ground by the truce the Party struck with him.
